<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Bishopric of Ösel-Wiek</h1><p class="st-meta">1228 CE – 1560 CE · capital Hapsal</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Prince-bishopric of western Estonia and its islands, ruled from Hapsal and Arensburg until its bishop sold it to Denmark in 1559-1560.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the conquest of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/saaremaa">Saaremaa</a> in 1227 and of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/laanemaa">Läänemaa</a>, a bishopric for the islands and the western coast was founded in 1228. Its lands were shared with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/livonian-order">Livonian Order</a>, which took parts of Saaremaa and the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhu">Muhu</a>, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> held most of Läänemaa and western Saaremaa.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> resided first at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lihula">Lihula</a>, then from the 13th century at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haapsalu">Hapsal</a> (Haapsalu), and also built the castle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuressaare">Arensburg</a> (Kuressaare) on <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/saaremaa">Saaremaa</a>. They governed through German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, a cathedral chapter and the diets of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/terra-mariana">Terra Mariana</a>. Repeated Estonian revolts, including the uprising of 1343, shook the islands.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Facing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-war">Livonian War</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-von-munchhausen">Bishop Johann von Münchhausen</a> sold the bishopric to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii-of-denmark">Frederick II of Denmark</a> in 1559, and the king passed it to his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-duke-of-holstein">Magnus of Holstein</a>, who arrived in 1560. It became <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/danish-osel">Danish Ösel</a>, and the mainland part fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/bishopric-of-osel-wiek.jpg" alt="Bishopric of Ösel-Wiek" width="320" height="347"><figcaption>Image: Termer · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Medieval_Livonia_1260.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1228 CE – 1560 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hapsal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Johann von Münchhausen</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Low German, Latin, Estonian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/estonia">Estonia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Prince-Bishopric of Ösel-Wiek, Saare-Lääne bishopric</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1394&amp;focus=bishopric-of-osel-wiek" aria-label="Bishopric of Ösel-Wiek on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5159 211 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bishopric of Ösel-Wiek lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5479 467L5539 500M5479 467L5229 694M5479 467L5274 535M5479 467L5478 463M5479 467L5502 450M5479 467L5521 437"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5539 500h0M5229 694h0M5274 535h0M5478 463h0M5502 450h0M5521 437h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5479 467h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5479 467h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1394&amp;focus=bishopric-of-osel-wiek">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
